# scene: kindly-opening
# set: ferry
# note: Ferry to fog to the spring. Ordinary, wrong, eerie, and then leaned on.

shot establish on deck.bow dist 8.15 pitch 9.2 az 181.4 lift 1.4 axis aperture
shot two-shot between player recruit dist 6.4 pitch 17 az 62 lift 1.2 axis actors bias 0.5
shot warning between player recruit dist 4.8 pitch 11 az 62 lift 1.3 axis actors bias 0.74
shot departure on deck.bow dist 8 pitch 30 az 0 lift 0.2 axis aperture
shot emptying on deck.bow dist 11 pitch 32 az 0 lift 0.15 axis aperture
shot horizon on deck.bow dist 11.5 pitch 11 az 0 lift 0.9 axis aperture
shot listening on deck.bow dist 8 pitch 8 az 0 lift 0.8 axis aperture
shot wake on player dist 8.2 pitch 33 az 14 lift 0.7 axis actors
shot pier on player dist 8.5 pitch 12 az 0 lift 1.4 axis actors
shot spring-wide between player keeper dist 13 pitch 16 az 0 lift 2.4 axis actors bias 0.58
shot folded on keeper dist 10.5 pitch 7 az 12 lift 2.6 axis aperture
shot keeper-rise on keeper dist 9 pitch -5 az 0 lift 2.8 axis aperture
shot keeper-close on keeper dist 9 pitch 2 az 16 lift 3 axis aperture
shot looming between player keeper dist 12 pitch -4 az 22 lift 2 axis actors bias 0.52
shot cornered between player keeper dist 7.6 pitch -3 az 40 lift 2.1 axis actors bias 0.3
shot burden on keeper dist 6 pitch 9 az -26 lift 2.9 axis aperture
shot threat on keeper dist 5.4 pitch 1 az 5 lift 3.4 axis aperture
shot farewell between player keeper dist 11.5 pitch 21 az 12 lift 1.6 axis actors bias 0.45

actor player at deck.player
actor recruit at deck.recruit facing deck.player
actor albert at office.counter
actor keeper at spring.keeper facing spring.player
actor passenger.a at seat.a
actor passenger.b at seat.b
actor passenger.c at seat.c

# ------------------------------------------------- ordinary
# Establish the working vessel before anything is taken away. Nothing
# supernatural can leave a world that was never shown as normal.
#
# She sits down. The Postmaster has had `seated_idle` and `sit_down` in her
# packet for as long as the recruit has had his, and the ferry used neither —
# so for the whole first half she stood on the deck of a passenger ferry, in
# front of a bench, talking to a man who was sitting on one. She stands again
# when the horizon goes, which is the first thing in the sequence worth
# standing up for.
#
# Albert is placed and hidden here and never referred to again. He is not in
# this sequence any more — he is the first thing the player finds once it lets
# go of her — but he still has to be standing behind his counter when she gets
# there, and `actor albert at office.counter` above is what puts him on his
# mark.
beat arrival
  @0 theme day
  @0 bed engine 0.85 1 over 2.4
  @0 hide albert
  @0 hide keeper
  @0 pose recruit seated_idle
  @0 pose player seated_idle
  @0 camera establish over 1.6
  @0 fade in 1.6
  @1.6 hold 1.4

beat greeting
  @0 sfx gull
  @0 camera two-shot over 1
  @0.35 pose recruit seated_talk_warm
  @0.4 say recruit recruitOrdinary

# The first thing the Postmaster ever says, and the player says it. Put here
# because the stakes are nothing: the mechanic gets taught on a beat where a
# wrong answer costs nothing, so that the two at the spring — where it is a very
# old thing leaning on her — are read rather than fumbled.
beat first-words
  @0 pose recruit seated_talk_warm
  @0 choice firstWords

beat recruit-answers
  @0 pose recruit seated_talk_warm
  @0 say recruit recruitReply

beat question
  @0 pose recruit seated_talk_warm
  @0 say recruit recruitQuestion

# The player names themselves and their island here, so the recruit's warning
# can quote the island back at them one beat later.
beat assignment
  @0 pose recruit seated_idle
  @0 ask assignment

# The one line the sequence turns on, so it is the one beat with its own
# push-in — and for a long time that push-in was pointed at the back of his
# head. Composed `on recruit` at a bare azimuth it put the camera outside the
# boat's rail: the rail ran across both faces at chest height, he was turned
# away from it because he is facing *her*, and the most important sentence in
# the first half played on his hood.
#
# It is the two-shot pushed in now — the same axis, the same side of the line,
# biased three quarters of the way onto him. Continuity comes free from reusing
# the angle the conversation has already been established on, and the only thing
# that changes between the beats is how close we are, which is what a push-in
# is supposed to be.
beat warning
  @0 camera warning over 0.9
  @0.3 pose recruit seated_concern
  @0.3 say recruit recruitWarning
  @0.3 mood recruit surprised 1.1

beat goodbye
  @0 camera establish over 1.1
  @0.15 pose recruit seated_talk_warm
  @0.2 say recruit lastGoodbye

# He leaves through the cabin door and is occluded by the cabin, rather than
# switched invisible in open frame. This is the last ordinary goodbye in the
# game and it was playing off-screen: there was a `departure` shot in the list,
# it was byte-identical to `emptying`, no cue ever called it, and the camera sat
# on the bow from the beat before while the only other person on the boat walked
# out of the story behind it.
#
# `departure` is now the close version of the frame the three stops repeat —
# same mark, same axis, eight metres instead of eleven. Deliberately a relation
# rather than a new angle: his exit is the first of four, and shooting it down
# the same line makes the three that follow read as the same thing happening
# again with fewer people each time.
#
# Both this and `emptying` used to be composed *on `cabin.door`*, and that is
# what made them unusable. The door is a mark inside a doorway, so the sightline
# solver moved the camera until it could see it — into the cabin, through the
# wall, with the door itself faded out by subject protection. Every shot down
# this deck now frames an open mark and lets the door sit in the background
# where it belongs.
beat recruit-leaves
  @0 camera departure over 1.2
  @0 pose recruit stand_up
  @0.75 move recruit to cabin.door over 1.7
  @2.45 move recruit to cabin.offstage over 1.2
  @3.65 hide recruit
  @3.65 layer counter 0.35 1.2
  @3.65 hold 1.1

# ------------------------------------------------- emptying
# The same framing at every stop, so the only thing that changes between them
# is how many people are left.
#
# The timings are *not* the same, and that is the point. Three identical beats
# played back to back read as a loop rather than as a passage of time — the eye
# learns the rhythm on the second and stops watching on the third. Each stop is
# tighter than the last, so the sequence accelerates the way a journey does
# when you have stopped counting the hours.
#
# The score goes with the light. `theme dusk` lands on the second stop rather
# than the first or the last, so the turn is buried in the middle of the
# sequence where nobody is looking for it — a change on the first stop reads as
# a cue, and one on the last reads as an announcement.
#
# The light goes with them. `/story` asks this sequence to be "a gentle
# time-lapse" and until now the only thing that passed was the passenger count
# — the ferry emptied under a fixed mid-afternoon sun, so three stops read as
# three events rather than as an afternoon. Each stop now runs the world clock
# forward, and by the empty deck the sun is most of the way down. Nobody will
# notice it happening, which is the whole idea; they will notice that the boat
# got colder.
beat stop-one
  @0 camera emptying over 1
  @0.6 pose passenger.a standing
  @0.8 move passenger.a to cabin.door over 1.1
  @1.9 move passenger.a to cabin.offstage over 0.9
  @2.8 hide passenger.a
  @2.8 layer kit 0 0.9
  @0 clock 16.6 over 2.6

beat stop-two
  @0.9 sfx gull
  @0 camera emptying over 0.8
  @0.4 pose passenger.b standing
  @0.55 move passenger.b to cabin.door over 0.95
  @1.5 move passenger.b to cabin.offstage over 0.8
  @2.3 hide passenger.b
  @2.3 layer arp 0 0.8
  @0 clock 17.5 over 2.2
  @0.9 theme dusk

beat stop-three
  @0 camera emptying over 0.6
  @0.25 pose passenger.c standing
  @0.35 move passenger.c to cabin.door over 0.85
  @1.2 move passenger.c to cabin.offstage over 0.7
  @1.9 hide passenger.c
  @1.9 layer bass 0 0.7
  @0 clock 18.2 over 1.9

# Absence is the subject of this shot, framed on the same axis as the full
# deck so the empty frame rhymes with the crowded one. The hold is long — long
# enough to be uncomfortable — because the three stops before it were getting
# faster and this is where that stops.
# The last gull. Nothing on the island ever makes this sound, and the player
# will not notice that it stopped — they will notice, later and without being
# able to say why, that the quiet on St. Mystere is a different quiet.
beat empty-deck
  @2.1 sfx gull
  @0 camera emptying over 1.4
  @1.4 hold 2.4

# ------------------------------------------------- the fog
# Visibility loss is rendered scene fog. The veil is only ever a cut cover.
#
# The engine goes with it, and goes *wrong* rather than quiet. `/story` asks
# for an engine that stretches here, and a bed that only faded would read as
# somebody turning the volume down — so the rate falls to under half across
# four and a half seconds while the filter closes with it, and again to a fifth
# under the voice. What the player hears is the machine losing its top end,
# which is the same thing the horizon is doing.
#
# Two different jobs, and for a while one number was doing both badly.
#
# **On the boat**, the fog has to take the *horizon* — `/story` asks for exactly
# that image and nothing else in the beat carries it. A horizon is at infinity,
# so erasing it needs a genuinely tight far plane; at 46 the sea still met the
# sky in a crisp line and the beat had no subject. Nothing here needs to be
# legible except the deck and the person standing on it, and both are inside
# seven metres of their own camera, so 26 costs nothing.
#
# **On the dock and the walk**, the opposite. `DayNight` copies the sky into the
# fog colour every frame, so at dusk the fog is a *bright* salmon — closed to
# the crossing's numbers it put an opaque warm wash over everything, including
# the Postmaster eight metres from her own camera, and the frame the player was
# handed control on had nothing legible in it at all. So the fog opens again as
# she comes to. Navigation is the wisp's job by then, and the fog only has to
# take the island's far end away.
beat horizon-loss
  @0 pose player stand_up
  @0 bed engine 0.62 0.44 over 4.5
  @0 camera horizon over 2.2
  @0 layer lead 0.18 2.4
  @0 theme night
  @0.2 fog 7 26 2.6
  @2.8 hold 1.2

# A voice from nowhere, over a horizon that is no longer there. The beat had no
# camera cue at all, which left it as a dialogue box over a held frame — the one
# moment in the sequence where something is *reaching* for her, played with the
# camera parked.
#
# The push is slow, long and eased out: six seconds to cover three and a half
# metres, so it is still moving under the second line and has almost stopped by
# the time the box closes. Both ends sit further back than they want to on
# paper, because the Postmaster stands off the bow axis and close to the lens —
# framed for the *sea*, this shot puts the back of her head across a third of
# the picture. Nobody should be able to say the camera moved. They
# should only notice that the frame got smaller while somebody was talking.
beat voice
  @0 bed engine 0.3 0.2 over 5.5
  @0 camera listening over 6 ease out
  @0.4 say keeper fogVoice

# The cut from boat to dock. The veil covers it, the ferry is struck behind it,
# and the picture comes back on the berth — so the boat is not seen to vanish,
# which would be an effect rather than an absence.
# The last of the light goes under the veil, with the boat. She was not awake
# for it — which is the one piece of time in this sequence the player is not
# shown, and the only honest place to move a clock without showing it.
beat fog-cut
  @0 bed engine 0 0.12 over 1.4
  @0 fade out 1.2
  @1.2 strike ferry
  @1.2 clock 18.6 over 1.4

# ------------------------------------------------- the dock
# The boat is gone and the player comes to on the berth it was tied to.
#
# She is left lying for a beat before she is allowed up. The old staging had
# her on her feet six tenths of a second after the picture came back, which
# played as waking briskly from a nap rather than as coming to on a pier with
# no memory of arriving.
beat wake
  @0 fog 11 44 3
  @0 move player to dock.wake over 0
  @0 pose player lying
  @0 camera wake over 1.3
  @0 fade in 2.4
  @2.4 hold 1.4
  @3.8 pose player standing

# The narration names the light before the player is asked to follow it, and
# the light is already burning at the end of the pier when she looks up.
beat pier-light
  @0 camera pier over 1.6
  @0.6 wisp lead to spring.approach
  @1 say keeper dock

# The first real choice, and by a long way the longest beat in the sequence:
# forty-five seconds of walking, and until now forty-five seconds in which
# nothing whatsoever happened. The wisp holds station ahead of her the whole way
# — see `Wisp.ts` for why this is a light and not the trail of lamp posts it
# replaced — but a light that waits is company, not an event.
#
# So a bell rings. `/story` asks this walk for "a bell that rings without wind"
# and there was no bell in the game at all. It is unaccompanied, inharmonic and
# long, and it arrives three times: once alone, then twice close together much
# later, which is the pattern of something being struck rather than something
# keeping time. Nothing explains it and nothing ever will — it is the first
# evidence the player gets that this island is occupied.
#
# The offsets are wall-clock from the beat's start rather than tied to a place
# on the route, because the route is hers to walk at her own pace: a bell keyed
# to a position would ring early for a player who hurries and never at all for
# one who stops to look at the letters.
#
# They are also early. A cue that has not fired when `control` resolves never
# fires at all, and ninety-three metres is about twenty-five seconds for
# somebody walking it straight — so a pair scheduled at thirty-one seconds is a
# pair only the dawdlers ever hear. All three are inside the fastest plausible
# crossing now, and a slow walk simply gets more silence after them rather than
# a different scene.
beat walk-inland
  @0 control to spring.approach radius 3
  @8 sfx bell
  @20 sfx bell
  @21.4 sfx bell

# ------------------------------------------------- the Keeper
# Nothing is revealed here yet. The shape by the water is held in frame long
# enough to be seen and not long enough to be read, because the thing that
# tells the player what it is should be the light going into it.
# He is posed by *not* being posed. `keeper_unfold` is the only clip that could
# describe him before he wakes and it ends risen, so there is nothing to hold at
# a first frame; the bind pose is what the player sees, and it is upright. What
# says he is not awake is the ember: `OpeningStage` blacks it out, and nothing
# lights it until the wisp goes in.
beat spring-arrival
  @0 bed keeper 0.3 0.55 over 3.5
  @0 sfx page
  @0 move player to spring.player over 0
  @0 show keeper
  @0 camera spring-wide over 1.8
  @1.8 hold 1.2
  @1.8 say keeper spring

beat approach-the-shape
  @0 bed keeper 0.5 0.7 over 2
  @0 camera folded over 1.4
  @1.4 hold 1

# The payoff of the walk. The light the player followed for sixty metres is
# what he has been missing, and it goes *into* him rather than arriving beside
# him. `wisp into` blocks until the light is out, so the unfold below starts on
# the frame it disappears in.
beat the-light-returns
  @0 camera keeper-rise over 2.2
  @0.4 wisp into keeper
  @1.9 sfx stinger
  @1.9 bed keeper 0.95 1 over 1.6
  @0 fog 14 60 2.6

beat unfold
  @0 pose keeper keeper_unfold
  @0.9 layer lead 0.42 2.2
  @1.4 camera keeper-close over 1.8
  @2.6 say keeper keeperWake

# It does not wake frightening; it wakes *uninterested*, and then it comes
# closer. Almost none of this beat is in the words — "you wear her coat" is four
# short lines of nothing much — and what carries it is that he crosses half the
# pool while he says them and she gives ground without being asked to. The
# camera drops below his eye line as he arrives, so the frame is looking up at
# him by the time he mentions the last one.
#
# The fog closes here rather than opening. It opened on this beat when he was a
# mourner and the scene was letting its held breath out; he is applying pressure
# now, so the world gets smaller around the two of them and stays small until
# she has agreed.
beat mistaken
  @0 pose keeper keeper_talk
  @0 camera looming over 3.4
  @0.2 fog 6 26 3
  @0.6 move keeper to spring.loom over 3.2
  @1.4 move player to spring.recoil over 1.1
  @1.4 face player keeper
  @1.4 say keeper keeperMistake

# Both of these are composed for where the actors *end up*, not where they
# start. `looming` and `cornered` were framed on the standing positions and
# played as an extreme close-up of his robes with the Postmaster off-screen
# entirely, because by the time either is on screen he has crossed half the pool
# and she has given a metre and a half of ground — five metres between them
# where the shot list assumed nine.
#
# Held at his new distance rather than pulled back out to a comfortable
# two-shot. He has closed on her, and the scene does not give her the room back
# until he has what he came for. `cornered` sits behind her shoulder and below
# his, which is the only frame in the game composed to make the player small.
#
# Its `lift` is shorter than the geometry wants, as `looming` and `farewell` are
# too, because the dialogue box owns the bottom fifth of the frame and aiming a
# shot lower lifts its content up out of the way. Composed on the geometry alone
# these three put the Postmaster exactly where the words she is listening to
# would cover her.
# She answers him, from the ground she has just been backed onto. Three ways to
# take it — frightened, curious, defiant — and he has something for each. The
# beat after this exists only to say it back to her, because a choice nobody
# answers is a choice the player correctly reads as decoration.
beat stand-ground
  @0 camera cornered over 2.2
  @0.4 choice standGround

beat he-answers
  @0 pose keeper keeper_talk
  @0 say keeper keeperReply

# Nobody says anything here. Four fourteen-second talking beats were running
# back to back through the climax — `mistaken`, `who-he-is`, `the-backlog`,
# `the-asking` — which is a minute of the same rhythm at the one point in the
# sequence where the rhythm should be doing the work. The emptying ferry got a
# tempo and this did not.
#
# So the beat between his answer and his account of himself is silent, close,
# and off his axis: the letters pinned to him and turning around him, held long
# enough for the player to read what he is carrying before he explains it. It
# is also the cheapest possible version of the thing this whole revision is
# for — the answer arriving before the question is asked out loud.
beat the-burden
  @0 camera burden over 2.6
  @2.6 hold 1.6

beat who-he-is
  @0 pose keeper keeper_talk
  @0.3 say keeper keeperWho

beat the-backlog
  @0 pose keeper keeper_talk
  @0 say keeper keeperBacklog

# The one sentence where he says what happens if she refuses, given its own
# beat and its own push-in. It was the back half of a two-line block, arriving
# mid-paragraph at the same size as the exposition around it.
beat the-threat
  @0 camera threat over 1.6
  @0.5 say keeper keeperThreat

# The score comes back with the air. `dusk` is the theme the ferry emptied
# into — warm, a touch melancholy, the one the sequence lost when the fog took
# it — so bringing it back on the beat she agrees says the pressure is over
# without anybody saying so, and says it in the register the *ordinary* world
# was in. She has not escaped him; she has been let go.
#
# The parts come back the way they left: one at a time, staggered, and not all
# the way. Bass and arp were dropped a passenger at a time across the three
# stops and the arrangement has been down to a single line since the fog, so
# restoring them in one cue would announce the ending. `arp` stops at half —
# the ferry's version of this had it whole, and this island does not.
#
# He gets what he wanted, and the world gives her the air back: he withdraws to
# where he was standing, the fog opens all the way out, and the frame widens off
# the two of them onto the island she has just agreed to work. None of that is
# stated. The release *is* the statement — the pressure was applied deliberately
# and it stops the instant it has done its job, which is the last thing this
# scene tells the player about him.
beat the-asking
  @0 bed keeper 0.4 0.85 over 4
  @0 camera farewell over 2.8
  @0.4 pose keeper keeper_summon
  @0.4 move keeper to spring.keeper over 2.6
  @0.6 say keeper keeperAsk

# She takes the work on her own terms, or on his. Both accept — the opening has
# one exit and a branch that changed that would need a second island behind it —
# but the terms are hers, and his last line is an answer to the ones she set.
beat the-bargain
  @0 choice theBargain

beat struck
  @0 bed keeper 0.12 0.7 over 5
  @0 theme dusk
  @0 layer lead 1 3
  @0.4 layer bass 0.85 3.5
  @0.9 layer arp 0.5 4
  @0 pose keeper keeper_talk
  @0 say keeper keeperClose
  @0 fog 40 150 3.5

beat handoff
  @0 hold 1.2
  @1.2 title KINDLY FORWARD
